<p>During my college days, back when I had to come up with headlines for our newspaper, we used the AP Stylebook. I roughly remember that words that don&rsquo;t have meaning of their own were lowercased and meaningful words got capitalized. This is more or less what I&rsquo;ve done here, on my blog.</p>
<p>The title of this post, &ldquo;How do you Title your Posts,&rdquo; is an example: &ldquo;How&rdquo; is the first word, so it gets capitalized (beginning of sentence). &ldquo;Do&rdquo; and &ldquo;you&rdquo; are meaningless on their own; they just glue to other parts, so lowercase. &ldquo;Title&rdquo; and &ldquo;Posts&rdquo; are the main parts, so they stand on their own. The connecting word between them, &ldquo;your,&rdquo; is what links them, so lowercase. Another way to look at this is that if I were to title this post &ldquo;How Title Posts,&rdquo; it still conveys a meaning — but &ldquo;how do you your&rdquo; is gibberish.</p>
<p>If you find this explanation confusing and think I make this up as I go, you&rsquo;re right. That&rsquo;s why my blog&rsquo;s titles are messy. I want to do something about it, to come up with some guide to follow.</p>
<p>We had <a href="https://michaelharley.net/posts/2026/06/15/bloggers-can-we-make-better-titles-for-our-posts/">a conversation around the <em>content</em> of blog titles</a>, but not syntax. From what I can tell, we bloggers capitalize titles in mostly three different ways. If I use my title here as an example again:</p>
<ol>
<li>How do you title your posts</li>
<li>How Do You Title Your Posts</li>
<li>How Do you Title your Posts</li>
</ol>
<p>I don&rsquo;t like to capitalize just the first word (1: sentence case). It feels lazy to me. Not every post on my blog gets a title, so posts that do need to have presence, not look like they&rsquo;re apologizing for being late to the party. You may disagree, and plenty of blogs choose this route.</p>
<p>The next one (2: title case) is slightly better, but not by much. It feels forced in a way, because some of the small words are used as connecting glue: they have no power on their own and shouldn&rsquo;t be capitalized.</p>
<p>The advantage of the first two methods is that they&rsquo;re consistent and easy to follow. When you start playing around with which words get capitalized and why, as we have in 3, things get complicated and inconsistent.</p>
<p>I went back to check the <a href="https://archive.org/download/the-associated-press-ap-stylebook-2020/The%20Associated%20Press%20-%20AP%20Stylebook%202020-2022-The%20Associates%20Press%20%282020%29.pdf">AP Stylebook says</a>:<sup><a id="fnr.1" class="footref" href="#fn.1" role="doc-backlink">1</a></sup></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Capitalize only the first word and proper nouns in headlines that use AP style. Exception:
The first word after a colon is always uppercase in headlines. Always capitalize the first letter of a headline, even if it starts with a proper name such as iPhone or eBay, though recasting may be the better choice.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ugh!</p>
<p>First, it takes me back to grammar school. Fine, proper nouns are not that hard, they are basically names: Mount Everest is a proper noun, mountain is not. But then I also need to remember to capitalize the first letter after a colon, and what I especially don&rsquo;t like is to capitalize iPhone as IPhone if it&rsquo;s the first word in the title. It looks so of that they even suggest avoiding it and finding a workaround&hellip; So why bother?</p>
<p>Besides, these guides mean that most of my titles will end up having only the first word capitalized. Here are a few random titles from my blog: &ldquo;My blog fails accessibility tests, and that&rsquo;s okay&rdquo; or &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t use soap for all my dishes.&rdquo; This feels especially off with series titles, like my Emacs Config Gems. Writing those as &ldquo;Emacs Config Gems - Part X&rdquo; is good. &ldquo;Emacs config gems - part x&rdquo; is not (technically I could argue an em dash is like a colon, so the word &ldquo;part&rdquo; is capitalized, which is slightly better, but it feels like a hack to me).</p>
<p>At this point, I&rsquo;m basically where I started. The words that seem important — for reasons I cannot justify with any official guides — get capitalized. Call it the unsupported title case&hellip; case.</p>
